-- title:
--   Lifting residues integral over the ̄ u⁻¹-chart
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, a field $k$ of characteristic $q$ and a ring homomorphism $red : A \to k$; fix modular polynomial data `data` for $q$ (a monic bivariate integral polynomial of degree $\psi(q)$ annihilating the pair $(j,j_q)$) satisfying the Kronecker congruence `hKr`, together with integrality hypotheses $h\alpha$, $h\beta$ asserting that the ring homomorphisms underlying `heckeAlphaBar` and `heckeBetaBar` at level $1$ and prime $q$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ are integral, and a place specialisation $P$ of these data. Let $R$ be a level-one prolongation pair for $P$, so in particular $R$ carries two regular prolongations $R_1$, $R_2$ of $A$ to $F :=$ `modularFunctionFieldBar (1 * q)` with residue field $\bar F :=$ `modularFunctionFieldFullC (ResidueField A) 1`, subject to the compatibilities recorded in the structure. Write $u \in F$ for the element whose Laurent expansion is the image under `coeffEmb` of `modularUnitSeries (1 * q)` $= \Delta \cdot \Delta_q^{-1}$, and assume $u^{-1}$ lies in the valuation subring $R_1$.integers, with residue $\overline{u^{-1}} \in \bar F$. Then for every $h \in \bar F$ which is integral over the $\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$-subalgebra of $\bar F$ generated by $\overline{u^{-1}}$, there exists $f$ in $R_1$.integers such that $f$, viewed in $F$, is integral over the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-subalgebra of $F$ generated by $u^{-1}$, and $R_1$.residue $f = h$.
--
--   This is the residue-surjectivity statement on the affine chart cut out by the inverse modular unit $u^{-1}$ on $X_0(q)$: every residue integral over the corresponding chart downstairs is hit by a function integral over $\overline{\mathbb Q}[u^{-1}]$. It serves as the chart hypothesis in the Deuring-style comparison of the two prolongations, and is used by [`ModularCurve.PlaceSpecialization.LevelOneProlongationPair.divisorLawFst_oneSided`](thm.html#ModularCurve.PlaceSpecialization.LevelOneProlongationPair.divisorLawFst_oneSided) and by the lemmas producing points where the reduction of $u^{-1}$ becomes non-unital or vanishes.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_PlaceSpecialization_LevelOneProlongationPair_exists_isIntegral_and_residue_eq_of_isIntegral_adjoin_residue_modularUnitSeries_inv.lean

